1b56|n|The act of moving; change of place or posture; transference, by any means, from one situation to another; natural or appropriate motion; progress; advancement; as, the movement of an army in marching or maneuvering; the movement of a wheel or a machine; the party of movement. ; Motion of the mind or feelings; emotion. ; Manner or style of moving; as, a slow, or quick, or sudden, movement. ; The rhythmical progression, pace, and tempo of a piece. ; One of the several strains or pieces, each complete in itself, with its own time and rhythm, which make up a larger work; as, the several movements of a suite or a symphony. ; A system of mechanism for transmitting motion of a definite character, or for transforming motion; as, the wheelwork of a watch.	a:f3j,54,6;169b,50,9;1vfo,43,6;1vl9,35,3;15bd,26,3~169b,50,9;f3j,54,6;1vfo,43,6;1vl9,35,3;15bd,26,3	n:1vld,59,3;x0g,38,10;5tw,38,10;29i1,32,6;1b2a,23,6;m21,13,4~5tw,38,10;x0g,38,10;29i1,32,6;1b2a,23,6;m21,13,4;1vld,59,3	N:5tw,28,5;1wkt,22,7~1wkt,22,7;5tw,28,5
